COMMAND "Classify a PortableDoc Block Type" DESCRIPTION "Add a block type to its composition-doctrine tier word list in api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex — the step add-block-type could only describe as manual prose (its conditional step 1, the D11 gap). One self-consuming REPLACE of the chosen tier's ~w( opener line: a scaffy provenance comment above it plus the opener re-emitted with the block type as the list's first word. Tier is the first ONEOF enum variable in the corpus (D56): the engine refuses any --var Tier outside element|widget|section at substitution time, before anything is written." LAST_UPDATED "17-07-2026-00-00-00" DOMAIN "barkpark" TAGS "portabledoc", "block" CONCEPT "block-type" VARIANT "tier-classify" DIRECTION "add" VARIABLES
  VARIABLE 1 "BlockName" TITLE "The block type to classify" DESCRIPTION "The PortableDoc `type` string of the block being classified, referenced in its kebab spelling {{.block-name}} throughout — it is the literal word planted into the ~w list, the same string compose_block/2 dispatches on. Classify the SAME spelling add-block-type scaffolded; tiers_test.exs cross-checks the two surfaces byte-for-byte." EXAMPLES "timeline", "fact-box", "milestone"
  VARIABLE 2 "Tier" ONEOF "element", "widget", "section" TITLE "The composition-doctrine tier" DESCRIPTION "Which word list the block joins (tiers.ex, composition-doctrine ladder): element = a single-purpose leaf, widget = composes elements into ONE purposeful unit, section = owns its children's LAYOUT. ONEOF is the runtime wall (D56): any other value is a usage error at substitution — exit 2, nothing written. Referenced in its lowercase spelling {{.tier}} so the value lands verbatim in `@element` / `@widget` / `@section`. NOTE the section caveat in the prose below: today's @section list is one-line, so a :section classification fails loud at the anchor instead of applying." EXAMPLES "element", "widget", "section"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS COMMAND HONESTLY DOES (closes the D11 'conditional/manual' gap)
#
#   ○ api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex   injected — provenance comment +
#     {{.block-name}} planted as the FIRST word of the @{{.tier}} ~w list
#
# add-block-type's prose step 1 told you to hand-edit tiers.ex; this command IS
# that edit. Word order inside a ~w list is non-semantic (the module folds the
# lists into set maps, tiers.ex:88-89), so first-word placement is safe and
# gives the op a stable, templatable anchor: the list's opener line.
#
# PAIRING LAW — run AFTER add-block-type (or any change that gives the type a
# compose_block/2 clause). tiers_test.exs pins completeness in BOTH directions
# (test/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers_test.exs "completeness vs. the render
# surface"): every renderable type must have a tier AND Tiers must not claim a
# type the reader cannot produce. A word planted here without its compose
# clause reds that second direction — which is why the mix gate below is
# TIER ci: it goes green exactly when the pair has landed together.
#
# ANCHOR REALITY (D5, grep-proven at authoring time):
#   @element ~w(   tiers.ex:57 — multi-line list, opener line matches once
#   @widget ~w(    tiers.ex:75 — multi-line list, opener line matches once
#   @section       tiers.ex:86 — ONE-LINE list: `  @section ~w(section columns)`
# The fenced anchor is the bare opener LINE `  @{{.tier}} ~w(`, so Tier=section
# clean-MISSES today's one-line shape: the engine refuses loud (D20 — anchor
# occurs 0 times, run aborts, NOTHING written). That is honest fail-closed
# behaviour, kept deliberately: new :section types are rare (the tier holds
# exactly section + columns), and silently rewriting a one-line list risks the
# exact accretion corruption D20 exists to block. If you genuinely add a third
# :section type, hand-edit tiers.ex:86 (and consider breaking the list open to
# the multi-line shape, which makes this command apply to it thereafter).
#
# WHY A SELF-CONSUMING REPLACE (the D58 idiom, not an INSERT): a comment cannot
# live INSIDE a ~w( sigil (every token in the parens is a word), so the mark
# line must sit ABOVE the opener — and the only byte-exact, line-granular
# anchor for "above the opener" is the opener line itself. The opener survives
# as a substring of its own payload, so E-005 requires BOTH the payload-derived
# ASLONG below (idempotency: a re-run with the same block skips) and a REANCHOR.
#
# RE-RUN MATRIX (per tier family, mark prefix tier-{{.tier}}-):
#   * same block, same tier   → ASLONG guard `~w({{.block-name}}` already
#     present → op skips (idempotent, D23).
#   * new block, DIFFERENT tier → that tier's family has no mark yet → fresh
#     run-1 anchor on its own opener line → clean apply.
#   * new block, SAME tier → REANCHOR's run-2 splice asserts the family tail
#     ends in the template's closing byte; this template's tail is the mutated
#     opener (ends in the block word), so the check FAILS LOUD and nothing is
#     written (D33 tail-desync refusal — the splice's comma separator would
#     corrupt a ~w list, so refusing is correct). Classify the second
#     same-tier block by hand, or `bp scaffy remove` the first and re-run both.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The one op. Target = the chosen tier's opener line, byte-exact with its
# 2-space indent (tiers.ex:57/:75). Payload = provenance comment planting the
# real mark (D24 — MARK:<name> verbatim in the payload) + the opener re-emitted
# with {{.block-name}} as the first list word (the original word lines that
# follow are untouched — ~w( spans lines). ASLONG derives from the payload
# (`~w({{.block-name}}` is text this op writes) with add polarity (E-011);
# REANCHOR keys the per-tier mark family (E-005 pair).
IN "api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex"
### Plant {{.block-name}} into the @{{.tier}} word list
REPLACE
::: {{.tier}} list opener :::
  @{{.tier}} ~w(
::: {{.tier}} list opener :::
WITH
::: {{.tier}} opener plus {{.block-name}} :::
  # scaffy:classify-block-type {{.block-name}} MARK:tier-{{.tier}}-{{.block-name}}
  @{{.tier}} ~w({{.block-name}}
::: {{.tier}} opener plus {{.block-name}} :::
MARK "tier-{{.tier}}-{{.block-name}}"
REANCHOR "tier-{{.tier}}-"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "~w({{.block-name}}"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTCONDITIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The block word sits directly inside the RIGHT tier's list opener, and the
# provenance mark is planted. Both strings are verbatim substrings the op
# writes (A4/A5: ASCII, no }} beside a literal brace).
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex" CONTAINS "@{{.tier}} ~w({{.block-name}}"
ASSERT FILE "api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex" CONTAINS "MARK:tier-{{.tier}}-{{.block-name}}"

# LOCAL: the cheap litter-immune placement proof — the word is on the chosen
# tier's opener line, not merely somewhere in the file.
ASSERT CMD "grep -q '@{{.tier}} ~w({{.block-name}}' api/lib/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers.ex"

# TIER ci: the completeness invariant itself — EVERY renderable type has a
# tier AND Tiers claims nothing unrenderable. Green exactly when this run is
# paired with the block's compose clause (see PAIRING LAW above). Deferred to
# CI: this worktree-agnostic Elixir suite is not on the proven local-safe list.
ASSERT CMD "cd api && mix test test/barkpark/portable_doc/tiers_test.exs" TIER ci
